Task 1: Environment Setup and Tool Installation
Subtask 1.1: Install Required Tools
First, let's install all the necessary tools on your Linux machine.

Step 1: Update the system package manager

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
Step 2: Install curl and other essential tools

sudo apt install -y curl wget apt-transport-https ca-certificates gnupg lsb-release
Step 3: Install Docker (required for Kubernetes)

curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg

echo "deb [arch=amd64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io

sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker
Step 4: Install kubectl

curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
Step 5: Install Helm

curl https://baltocdn.com/helm/signing.asc | gpg --dearmor | sudo tee /usr/share/keyrings/helm.gpg > /dev/null
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/helm.gpg] https://baltocdn.com/helm/stable/debian/ all main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/helm-stable-debian.list
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y helm
Step 6: Install and start Minikube

curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64
sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube

minikube start --driver=docker
Subtask 1.2: Verify Installation
Step 1: Verify all tools are installed correctly

docker --version
kubectl version --client
helm version
minikube status
Step 2: Configure kubectl to use minikube context

kubectl config use-context minikube
kubectl get nodes
Task 2: Add Dependencies to a Helm Chart
Subtask 2.1: Create a Parent Chart
Step 1: Create a new directory for our lab work

mkdir -p ~/helm-dependencies-lab
cd ~/helm-dependencies-lab
Step 2: Create a new Helm chart that will serve as our parent chart

helm create webapp-with-deps
cd webapp-with-deps
Step 3: Examine the default chart structure

tree .
Subtask 2.2: Understanding Chart Dependencies
Dependencies in Helm allow you to include other charts as components of your chart. This is useful when your application requires multiple services like databases, caches, or monitoring tools.

Step 1: View the current Chart.yaml file

cat Chart.yaml
Step 2: Create a backup of the original Chart.yaml

cp Chart.yaml Chart.yaml.backup
Subtask 2.3: Add Dependencies to Chart.yaml
Step 1: Edit the Chart.yaml file to add dependencies

cat > Chart.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v2
name: webapp-with-deps
description: A Helm chart for a web application with dependencies
type: application
version: 0.1.0
appVersion: "1.0"

dependencies:
  - name: redis
    version: "17.15.6"
    repository: "https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami"
    condition: redis.enabled
  - name: postgresql
    version: "12.12.10"
    repository: "https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami"
    condition: postgresql.enabled
  - name: nginx
    version: "15.4.4"
    repository: "https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami"
    condition: nginx.enabled
EOF
Step 2: Create a values.yaml file to control dependency enablement

cat > values.yaml << 'EOF'
# Default values for webapp-with-deps
replicaCount: 1

image:
  repository: nginx
  pullPolicy: IfNotPresent
  tag: "1.21"

service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80

# Dependency configurations
redis:
  enabled: true
  auth:
    enabled: false
  master:
    persistence:
      enabled: false

postgresql:
  enabled: true
  auth:
    postgresPassword: "mypassword"
    database: "webapp"
  primary:
    persistence:
      enabled: false

nginx:
  enabled: false
  service:
    type: ClusterIP
EOF
Subtask 2.4: Update Dependencies
Step 1: Add the Bitnami repository to Helm

helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
helm repo update
Step 2: Download the dependencies

helm dependency update
Step 3: Verify that dependencies were downloaded

ls -la charts/
cat Chart.lock
The Chart.lock file contains the exact versions of dependencies that were downloaded, ensuring reproducible builds.

Subtask 2.5: Examine Dependency Structure
Step 1: Extract and examine one of the dependency charts

cd charts
tar -tf redis-17.15.6.tgz | head -20
Step 2: Return to the main chart directory

cd ..
Task 3: Install Dependent Charts Together
Subtask 3.1: Create a Custom Template
Step 1: Modify the main deployment template to connect with dependencies

cat > templates/deployment.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: {{ include "webapp-with-deps.fullname" . }}
  labels:
    {{- include "webapp-with-deps.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
spec:
  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      {{- include "webapp-with-deps.selectorLabels" . | nindent 6 }}
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        {{- include "webapp-with-deps.selectorLabels" . | nindent 8 }}
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: {{ .Chart.Name }}
          image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}"
          imagePullPolicy: {{ .Values.image.pullPolicy }}
          ports:
            - name: http
              containerPort: 80
              protocol: TCP
          env:
            {{- if .Values.redis.enabled }}
            - name: REDIS_HOST
              value: "{{ include "webapp-with-deps.fullname" . }}-redis-master"
            - name: REDIS_PORT
              value: "6379"
            {{- end }}
            {{- if .Values.postgresql.enabled }}
            - name: POSTGRES_HOST
              value: "{{ include "webapp-with-deps.fullname" . }}-postgresql"
            - name: POSTGRES_PORT
              value: "5432"
            - name: POSTGRES_DB
              value: "{{ .Values.postgresql.auth.database }}"
            {{- end }}
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /
              port: http
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /
              port: http
EOF
